From af0cdfd17ad4c4501a06ae5686a2f22bf0c0a9ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: port <port@localhost>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 21:27:38 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] 0014: take the die caps from the GC info table, not from a
 list of dies

gfx_default_caps() baked in the geometry of the two dies that had been
surveyed and 0003 added a branch to it for Navi 44, so any other RDNA4
die - or a harvested part - silently got one of two shapes it never had.
The caps feed the CU mask and the GFX ring, so a wrong number does not
merely misreport the card, it arms the compute stages against a die that
is not there.

Linux takes the same four caps from the GC info table in the discovery
binary this kext already reads (amdgpu_discovery.c:2173-2181); the port
parsed only the IP list. IpDiscovery::gcInfo() now parses that table into
IpGcInfo, DeviceContext carries it as gcTab, and gfx_gpu_early_init()
applies it - all five words or none, since a table whose SE count reads 0
while its CU count reads 8 is a table this code does not understand.
Out-of-range keeps the per-IP-version caps and logs which source won,
because that log is the only witness a hardware boot leaves.

TCC has no alternative source on this generation: gc_12_0_0_sh_mask.h has
no TCC field and upstream's gfx_v12_0_get_tcc_info() is an empty stub. So
native ABI 1.14 repurposes n48n_topo.reserved[0] (byte 80) as
num_tcc_blocks - no size change, no new selector, and a 1.13 client never
reads that byte. 0 means "this card filed no table", which a consumer
must read as keep-what-you-have rather than as zero TCCs.
